          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-20cf299d1bfdc4cf\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Lorck's insistent rhetorical question presents as true the proposition that he loves Constance as deeply as possible and presses her to acknowledge it. It is principally a truth-conditional avowal. The words themselves do not undertake a new relational commitment, and their emotional insistence supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Lorck may be sincere, self-deceived, or strategically reassuring, but this affects the contextual interpretation rather than the utterance's dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lorck directly addresses Constance with the rhetorical question, “Tror du ikke, jeg elsker dig saa højt som det er muligt?”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lorck makes the avowal while defending his capacity for genuine love after Constance has discovered his prior sexual relations. His question is insistently reassuring and also pressures her to accept his self-exculpatory distinction between past sexual conduct and present love. Constance responds wearily that she does not know, and the narration later reveals that she has ceased loving him, creating strong narrative irony without changing the avowal's core T force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Tror du ikke, jeg elsker dig saa højt som det er muligt?” (“Do you not believe that I love you as deeply as possible?”)\",\"supports\":\"The embedded proposition explicitly presents Lorck's maximal love for Constance as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says that he asked “indtrængende” (insistently), after arguing that earlier sexual relations had not impaired his ability to feel true love.\",\"supports\":\"The delivery and argumentative setting show emphatic reassurance and an effort to secure Constance's assent, but not a new undertaking or reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Constance answers wearily, “Jeg ved ikke,” and later reflects on Lorck's self-assurance and his failure to understand that she has ceased to love him.\",\"supports\":\"Her rejection and the narrative irony complicate the avowal's credibility and interpersonal effect but do not displace its truth-conditional mode.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck immediately reformulates his position as an assurance that she has received a love “saa frisk og god, som du kan ønske dig den.”\",\"supports\":\"The continuation confirms that the highlighted question functions as an avowal or assurance about the quality of his existing love, rather than as a fresh commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Lorck's pressure, defensiveness, possible self-deception, and Constance's alienation are adequately treated as contextual features; no residual core force requires P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied translation was used as an analytical aid, with claims checked against the provided source context.\"}"
        }
